1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:06,260
So, um, imagine this, a massive, like glossy IT supplier proposal just lands on your desk.

2
00:00:06,460 --> 00:00:08,580
Right. I mean, that happens all the time.

3
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:15,840
Yeah, and you oversee a major digital program in your ministry, and this proposal is promising a flawless, unified service.

4
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:19,580
But the real question is, um, before you sign that contract,

5
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:21,240
how do you actually know

6
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:22,820
what you are buying?

7
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:30,860
Exactly. Welcome to today's deep dive. We are unpacking exactly how to evaluate these complex IT supplier proposals.

8
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:36,540
Because if you get this wrong, you do not just get a bad system, you end up with systems that are,

9
00:00:37,140 --> 00:00:39,240
well, fragmented, siloed,

10
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:42,960
duplicated, or just incredibly costly to change.

11
00:00:43,340 --> 00:00:45,920
Or locked into one supplier forever, right?

12
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:49,740
Exactly. So let us start at the beginning of the project, the design phase.

13
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:53,920
The standard move for a supplier is to hand you an exhaustive list for approval, right?

14
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:59,120
Yeah, like a list of maybe eighty decisions or, you know, a massive data dictionary.

15
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:01,220
Which looks completely exhaustive.

16
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:04,680
It does look exhaustive, but relying on a list is dangerous.

17
00:01:05,420 --> 00:01:10,540
It is entirely impossible to review effectively because you simply cannot see what is missing.

18
00:01:10,920 --> 00:01:18,440
Wait, but if a one hundred and forty field data dictionary is like perfectly comprehensive, why is it so dangerous for an official to rely on it instead of a story?

19
00:01:19,020 --> 00:01:21,900
Well, because a list does not reveal the hidden dead ends.

20
00:01:22,460 --> 00:01:23,560
What do you mean by dead ends?

21
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:26,760
Uh, like a step in the process with no response

22
00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:31,080
or a person who just, um, disappears from the workflow altogether.

23
00:01:31,940 --> 00:01:37,220
A story, however, going step by step with the actual screens, it reveals all of that.

24
00:01:38,020 --> 00:01:41,240
Right. So it bridges the gap between the business side and the IT side.

25
00:01:41,580 --> 00:01:45,360
Exactly. And this is exactly what the reference architecture describes.

26
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:51,080
It points out that enterprise architecture documents are meant to close that exact communication gap.

27
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:53,820
So both sides can actually plan together.

28
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:57,700
Yes. A story with its screens does that job perfectly.

29
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:04,740
Okay, so we have a shared story. That brings us to rule two, which is measuring the design against a list the design did not write.

30
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:07,580
Right. Once you have that shared story,

31
00:02:08,259 --> 00:02:11,900
you have to ensure the proposed design actually covers everything your program needs.

32
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:17,820
Which sounds obvious, but grading a system based on its own design is kinda like letting a student write their own exam.

33
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:21,160
Yeah, I mean, of course, they are going to score one hundred percent.

34
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:30,960
Exactly. If the list of what must be covered is drawn up from the design itself, it will always look complete, even if it is missing half of what your minister actually

35
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:31,340
needs.

36
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:33,760
So the defense here is independence.

37
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:40,620
Yes. The list of what was asked for must be written in the customer's own words before any design even exists.

38
00:02:41,040 --> 00:02:44,040
Okay, so we have a shared story, an independent checklist.

39
00:02:44,440 --> 00:02:45,980
Now we move to the implementation phase.

40
00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:48,560
But there is still one major blind spot here.

41
00:02:49,260 --> 00:02:52,860
Rule three is you must end at least one check on the running system.

42
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:56,320
Because documents can agree with each other perfectly, right?

43
00:02:56,560 --> 00:02:57,320
Oh, absolutely. Okay.

44
00:02:57,460 --> 00:03:01,680
They can align perfectly and still describe a system that does not actually work.

45
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:04,800
So the paper reality does not match the digital reality.

46
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:12,040
Exactly. The final check has to involve a real person finishing a real task on the actual system the service will run on.

47
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:16,780
Let us look at how this plays out in Progressa, which just to be clear, is a fictional country.

48
00:03:17,260 --> 00:03:20,620
Right. A fictional country, but a very real world scenario.

49
00:03:21,420 --> 00:03:28,720
Their higher education quality authority, um, known as PHEQA, received a proposal with three major shortfalls.

50
00:03:29,360 --> 00:03:35,340
And they fixed them using these rules. So first, instead of signing a data dictionary of one hundred and forty fields, what did they do?

51
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:42,520
Well, PHEQA refused the list. They accepted stories and a clickable walkthrough instead.

52
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:43,300
Nice.

53
00:03:44,220 --> 00:03:46,740
And second, instead of requirements written from the design?

54
00:03:47,380 --> 00:03:52,040
They accepted a register of what PHEQA asked for before any design existed.

55
00:03:52,320 --> 00:03:54,160
And finally, the testing phase.

56
00:03:54,680 --> 00:04:04,480
Right. Instead of testing on the supplier's test server, acceptance ended when a real PHEQA officer finished a real task on PHEQA's own installation.

57
00:04:04,920 --> 00:04:07,120
Signed in through the National Identity Authority, right.

58
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:10,340
Exactly. They closed every single loophole.

59
00:04:10,740 --> 00:04:18,860
So before you sign that proposal on your desk, pinpoint exactly what your officials approve, what the design is measured against, and where testing ends.

60
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:23,060
And if any of these belong to the supplier, you know, write the clause to change it.

61
00:04:23,640 --> 00:04:25,460
Keep the control in your ministry's hands.

62
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:26,280
Always.

